    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>How Vaccines Work</h1>

        <p>Vaccines are used to protect people from dangerous diseases. When you get vaccinated against a particular disease, your immune system learns to recognize that specific germ and is prepared to fight it off if you ever encounter it again.</p>

        <p>The vaccine introduces a harmless part of the germ into your body in a way that makes your immune system react to it without causing illness. This reaction helps build immunity so that when you do come across the real germ later on, your immune system can quickly respond and stop the disease before it starts.</p>

        <p>This process is called immunization, and it's what allows us to be protected from many common illnesses like measles, mumps, and polio. The more people who get vaccinated for a certain disease, the less likely any one person will have to deal with it directly because they've been exposed to enough people who are already immune.</p>

        <p>It's important to note that while vaccinations help prevent serious health issues, they're not a guarantee that you'll never get sick. Some individuals may still contract an illness even after being vaccinated, but their chances of becoming severely ill or spreading the infection to others are much lower.</p>

        <p>Make sure to follow all instructions given by healthcare professionals regarding vaccination schedules and proper administration techniques to ensure maximum effectiveness and safety.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
